{"duration_api_ms":29717,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"73a1ec18-1f08-465f-85bc-cc600ef7db31","total_cost_usd":0.2110085,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9233,"cache_read_input_tokens":4705,"output_tokens":4320,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1556},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9233,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4320,"cache_read_input_tokens":4705,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9233,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9233},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":8246,"outputTokens":14,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008316,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4320,"cacheReadInputTokens":4705,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9233,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2026925,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1556,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# What the Reviews Don't Show: A Pre-Mammoth Test Plan\n\nThe supplied reviews don't tell us what is required or best for Mammoth, but their caveats point to four checks you can run yourself.\n\nAs of October 15, 2026\n\nMammoth is a multi-day carry, so gear that works for a short outing can still fail by day two. Most gear reviews are written under lighter, shorter or milder conditions than that. This piece does not rank gear. It reads the reviewers' own caveats and turns them into things you can test before the event. The Mammoth official page was not part of the evidence for this piece, so we leave required status for every item unspecified. Nothing here implies a requirement. The companion video is at [VIDEO LINK].\n\n## Check your click values\n\nAccurateShooter's reader recommends that you test how much movement your scope actually delivers per click, rather than trusting the turret markings. The method is a ruler box test with an assistant, who cranks on 36 indicated MOA, or 144 clicks. [03-c1] The article also quotes Bryan Litz saying that more than 50% of the time, error in trajectory prediction at long range is actually scope adjustment. [03-c1] That is a quoted opinion, not a measurement we can verify, but the test costs an afternoon and a helper. Do it before the event, when the result can still change your dope.\n\n## Load-test the pack you would actually carry\n\nRokslide's KUIU Icon 6 review is a useful case. The bag tested was the 3500 CI daypack, and the same review lists larger options, including a multi-day 6000 CI freighter. [03-c2] The reviewer made 1.2-mile loops with 510 vertical feet gained and lost. [03-c2] Our notes list 28 lb of day gear and an added 64 lb simulated load for those loops, but the excerpt we have does not show those figures, so check the review before leaning on them. [03-c2] Either way, our read is that the heavy-load results come from a short loop on a day-sized bag, not a multi-day carry in a multi-day bag. Rokslide is a backcountry hunting outlet that is ad-supported. [03-c7]\n\nRuck Authority's two GORUCK reviews show the same gap from another angle. According to our notes, the reviewer disclosed free product and affiliate links. He said the Rucker 5.0's padded waist belt is optional, that he did not have one, and that he did not test with it. [03-c3] He also reported slightly sore traps and thought a hip belt might ease that. [03-c3] On the GR1, he tested at 20 lb without the chest strap and hip belt that GORUCK sells separately. [03-c3] He said he didn't miss the chest strap at that weight, and he acknowledged that experienced ruckers say a sternum strap matters when the weight goes up. [03-c3]\n\nThe practical point is that the setups reviewed may not be the setups you would carry. If you plan to use a hip belt and chest strap, test with them, at your full loaded weight, for long enough that sore shoulders or traps show up.\n\n## Check bulk and temperature margin together\n\nTreeline Review's reviewer, whose site uses an affiliate model, said the REI Co-op Magma 30 took up a significant amount of space in her Gregory Amber 44 L pack. [03-c4] She also said she would not push it much further below freezing. [03-c4] On her coldest tested night she did not feel cold, but she was wearing a wool baselayer and a long-sleeve top. [03-c4] So her comfort result includes clothing, and the bag's bulk is a pack-space cost she had to absorb.\n\nArgali's Brad Brooks gives a useful question to ask about any bag's rating. On Vortex's podcast he said many companies advertise a limit or extreme rating, while he argues for listing the comfort rating. He added that even a 20-degree comfort rating won't suit every sleeper, because people run hot or cold. [03-c5] This is a manufacturer's view, hosted by a commercial publisher that is also a Mammoth sponsor, so treat it as a lens for reading ratings, not as evidence about any particular bag. [03-c5]\n\nThe test: pack the bag in the pack you will carry, with everything else, and see what it displaces. Then sleep in it at a temperature near your expected low, in the layers you would actually have.\n\n## Test app-dependent optics as a system\n\nRokslide's Tract Toric LRF review reports a weight of 2 lbs. 5.5 ounces with a stud, power from a single CR2 battery, and a phone app as a necessary go-between for combining its ranges with wind data from a connected wind meter. [03-c6] The reviewer also saw the diopter shift out slightly on one occasion. [03-c6] Over several days, that means a phone, a wind meter, a battery and a binocular all have to work together, and you carry the weight of all of them. Run the whole chain before you rely on it, including battery drain and phone use.\n\n## What this doesn't show\n\nNo Mammoth organizer or past-competitor account was supplied, and no review tested gear over multi-day team carries in Mammoth-like conditions. Most findings are single-reviewer accounts. Ruck Authority and Treeline disclose free product or affiliate models, Rokslide is ad-supported, and Argali and Vortex are commercial voices. The four tests above are our inferences from the reviewers' stated conditions and caveats. None of these sources tested those protocols. The packets are also excerpts, so passages we didn't see may qualify or contradict what is shown.\n\n## Takeaway\n\nOur read is that four checks are worth doing: scope click values, packs at full loaded weight with your own belt and strap setup, sleeping bag bulk and temperature margin in your own pack, and any app-dependent optic as a complete system. [03-c7] These are not verdicts on what is required or necessary. They are ways to find out whether gear that did fine in someone else's shorter, lighter test holds up under your load.\n\n## Sources\n\nClaim IDs used: 03-c1, 03-c2, 03-c3, 03-c4, 03-c5, 03-c6, 03-c7\n\n- 03-c1, 03-c7: AccurateShooter, \"How to Find Your Scope's True Click Values with Box Test,\" October 15, 2026. https://bulletin.accurateshooter.com/2026/10/how-to-find-your-scopes-true-click-values-with-box-test/ (independent)\n- 03-c2, 03-c7: Rokslide, \"KUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review,\" September 23, 2026. https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/ (practitioner; ad-supported)\n- 03-c3, 03-c7: Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: My First Week on the Trail,\" September 24, 2026, at 1:03 and 3:00. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVz1KOKvi-I (reviewer disclosed free product and affiliate links, per our notes)\n- 03-c3: Ruck Authority, \"GORUCK GR1 26L Review: An Everyday Bag That Can Ruck,\" October 2, 2026, at 2:05. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlW6o3cO2rk (same disclosure)\n- 03-c4, 03-c7: Treeline Review, \"REI Co-op Magma 30 Sleeping Bag Review,\" October 15, 2026. https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review (affiliate model)\n- 03-c5: Vortex, \"#10MinuteTalk | Down Insulation Explained with Argali's Brad Brooks,\" October 6, 2026, at 27:01 and 27:33. http://www.vortexoptics.com (manufacturer-hosted podcast; Vortex is a Mammoth sponsor; Argali is a manufacturer)\n- 03-c6: Rokslide, \"Tract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review,\" October 10, 2026. https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/ (practitioner; ad-supported)\n\nThis article was drafted with AI assistance from cited sources and reviewed by a human editor.","ttft_ms":11900,"type":"result","duration_ms":28937,"uuid":"a5aaeaf1-1e75-49f2-8e03-ee92d39522c3","ttft_stream_ms":1044,"time_to_request_ms":111,"first_content_frame_ms":1045,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
